# Isolated implementation memory comparison

No memory workload has run. Earlier reports loaded original, baseline and candidate into each child, so their RSS/heap figures could not isolate an implementation. This prospective protocol gives each measured process exactly one selected reducer module and its own required dependencies. An original-only process legitimately includes released lodash/registry code; an application-only process loads only that selected application module. The result measures reducer-component process footprint, not a full Grafana application or whole product.

All original archive/module/dependency authentication and expected-result generation occur in separate, unmeasured preflight processes. Preflight saves complete input specifications, reducer ID arrays and independently serialized original results/cache states; it never asks a measured child to import a second reducer. The serializer must preserve undefined properties/array holes, signed zero, NaN/infinities, own keys and result/cache values. Hashes bind exact manifests, original package/dependency bytes and each selected module. The controller verifies source bytes immediately before/after every child. A measured worker hashes only its selected source files, validates module resolution and records loaded original CJS module paths when applicable; it does not read/decompress full archives or retain other implementation code.

Use sequential fresh Node v24.16.0 processes with identical executable/flags/environment, rotating original/baseline/candidate order and seven samples per workload. Record executable SHA-256, full version/V8/platform/architecture, command line, PID, exact source/fixture/expected hashes, wall intervals and raw observations. Run only after a separately confirmed quiet interval. Avoid Worker threads: they share process RSS and do not provide the desired process isolation. Loading one implementation is necessary but still leaves allocator/JIT/OS variability.

Measure three separate views:

1. Cold footprint: sample process memory before import, after selected module initialization plus consistent explicit GC, then after creating the shared input. Report raw snapshots and paired deltas, including negative/noisy values. Keep input creation separate from module-load cost.
2. Warm transient workload: warm only the selected implementation, release warmup results, apply the same GC protocol, then use fresh field/state objects per call with a shared values array. Retain only the final result/cache until the ending observation. Record memory at block boundaries and process-lifetime high-water RSS. This includes ordinary result/cache allocations and scratch arrays, but sampling may miss short peaks. Memory instrumentation runs separately from uninstrumented timings.
3. Deliberately retained results: retain a fixed number of field/state objects and returned results over the same shared input, then sample after GC. After clearing every retained result/state reference and collecting again, record the remaining footprint. This distinguishes caller retention from reclaimable scratch work; it does not equate a heap difference with a leak.

Use the existing deterministic 100/25,000-sample inputs and five reducer selections, with one fixed manifest shared across implementations. Prospective bounds are below; counts are chosen for a practical original-program runtime and must be frozen before execution. One retained field can own both a returned object and a distinct cache object; the worker must not merge them for measurement convenience.

| Workload | Warmup calls | Transient calls | Retained fields | Shared input samples |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Small standard | 20,000 | 100,000 | 10,000 | 100 |
| Large standard | 500 | 2,000 | 10,000 | 25,000 |
| Single p95 | 100 | 200 | 256 | 25,000 |
| All 99 percentiles | 2 | 16 | 16 | 25,000 |
| All 124 calculations | 2 | 16 | 16 | 25,000 |

These counts are identical for all implementations. The small retained-count controls for percentile workloads may be too small to distinguish output-object footprint from noise; report that gap instead of increasing counts only for a favorable implementation. The all-calculation output legitimately includes value arrays and its retained memory is therefore a different contract from standard or p95. The shared input does not model independently owned input arrays per dashboard field. A separate empty-controller/no-module control can characterize controller/input costs, but must not be silently subtracted from inclusive application results.

Collect `rss`, `heapTotal`, `heapUsed`, `external`, `arrayBuffers` and `resourceUsage().maxRSS`, distinguishing bytes from KiB. RSS covers resident native/runtime/code memory; heap figures cover V8, and arrayBuffers are included in external. `memoryUsage()` can add measurement overhead. `maxRSS` is lifetime high-water history, not a resettable allocation counter; do not subtract baseline high-water values and call the result a workload peak. Sampling and high-water RSS do not establish total allocated bytes. These definitions are grounded in the pinned [Node v24.16.0 process documentation](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.16.0/docs/api/process.html#processmemoryusage), preserved with complete SHA-256 and line locations in `source-inspection.json`.

Use the same explicit GC count and event-loop settling turns in every process. GC-induced observations are a diagnostic control, not normal application behavior or proof that OS pages were released; [`--expose-gc` is experimental](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.16.0/docs/api/cli.html#--expose-gc). If allocation attribution becomes necessary, run separate instrumented heap-profile observations and preserve the profiler data; the [Inspector heap-profiler interface](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.16.0/docs/api/inspector.html#heap-profiler) adds work and must not be combined with selected timing results.

Cap each child at 120 seconds and a 256 MiB old-space limit; the latter is not a total-RSS bound. The controller monitors only its owned child PID and terminates it above a declared 512 MiB RSS threshold, preserving the failure and observations. A limit-hit run is censored/incomplete, not evidence of zero memory or a performance win. Bound fixture/result/log sizes and preserve failed source hashes. No real inputs, credentials, accounts or user data are involved.

Before recording observations, compare a fresh calculation and its cache to the expected manifest; compare the last transient result and each retained result/cache after measurement, outside measurement snapshots. Prior full differential acceptance remains required for the exact candidate. Verify input arrays remain unchanged and preserve actual returned/cache identities: top-level objects are distinct in a fresh calculation, while allValues/uniqueValues array properties may share references with cache through the original shallow copy. Do not manufacture deep clones or deduplicate objects for measurement. Do not combine profiler, validation or hashing allocations with workload snapshots.

Report medians and complete sample ranges for inclusive cold RSS, post-GC heap retained deltas, transient snapshots and lifetime peak RSS as distinct outcomes. Preserve all raw samples and limit failures. No significance claim, whole-product resource conclusion, guaranteed lower memory or universal superiority follows from seven finite samples. A timing regression cannot be justified by unmeasured memory savings, and a smaller component footprint does not establish full product readiness.
